        { "TempC": 150, "Value": 98.85 },
        { "TempC": 200, "Value": 84.55 },
        { "TempC": 250, "Value": 73.45 },
        { "TempC": 300, "Value": 64.64 },
        { "TempC": 400, "Value": 51.61 },
        { "TempC": 500, "Value": 42.51 }
      ],
      "DensityKgM3": 6150,
      "SpecificHeat": 490,
      "Source": "Relative permittivity 9.5: wurtzite GaN's static value perpendicular to the c axis, 10.4 along it (Barker and Ilegems, Infrared lattice vibrations and free-electron dispersion in GaN, Phys. Rev. B 7, 743, 1973); Levinshtein et al. compile 8.9. It holds from DC to well above 100 GHz: the lattice resonance that ends it is in the infrared. Loss tangent 0.001: a representative value for a semi-insulating buffer, not a measurement - an epitaxial stack's loss is set by its buffer's doping. Thermal conductivity k(T) = k300 (T / 300 K)^-1.4 with k300 = 160 W/(m K), tabulated 20 to 500 C: representative of an epitaxial layer a few micrometres thick on SiC, between the 130 W/(m K) of Levinshtein, Rumyantsev and Shur (eds.), Properties of Advanced Semiconductor Materials (Wiley, 2001) and the ~230 W/(m K) of low-dislocation bulk crystal (Mion, Muth, Preble and Hanser, Appl. Phys. Lett. 89, 092123, 2006); the exponent is of the size reported for GaN above room temperature. Density 6150 kg/m3 and specific heat 490 J/(kg K): Levinshtein et al. Representative, not a measurement.",
      "Color": "#6f86b0"
    },
    {
      "Name": "CVD diamond",
      "Epsr": 5.7,
      "TanD": 0.0002,
      "Mur": 1,
      "ThermalK": 1800,
      "DensityKgM3": 3515,
      "SpecificHeat": 510,
      "Source": "Relative permittivity 5.7: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics (diamond). Diamond has no infrared-active lattice resonance, so this holds from DC to well above 100 GHz. Loss tangent 0.0002: a representative value for thermal-management grades, not a measurement; optical grades are far lower. Thermal conductivity 1800 W/(m K): a representative thermal-management grade of polycrystalline CVD diamond, which is sold from roughly 1000 to 2000 W/(m K); a constant is used because diamond is a spreader held near the heat sink's temperature. Density 3515 kg/m3 and specific heat 510 J/(kg K) at 25 C: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics.",
      "Color": "#d6e6ee"
    },
